 ## Overview
-This method implements the Marching Cubes isosurface extraction algorithm using a GPU Compute Shader, specifically designed for integration within the Unity engine [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of real-time surface generation from volumetric data by offloading the voxel processing and triangle generation to the GPU, while maintaining a CPU-based density field generation for demonstration purposes [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its siblings primarily in its use of an `AppendStructuredBuffer` for dynamic vertex emission and `Graphics.DrawProceduralIndirect` for rendering, relying on the parallel dispatch and lookup logic defined in [GPU Acceleration and Parallel Pipelines](4136-gpu-acceleration-and-parallel-pipelines.md) and [Marching Cubes Lookup and Configuration](2518-marching-cubes-lookup-and-configuration.md).
+This method implements the Marching Cubes isosurface extraction algorithm entirely on the GPU using a compute shader, typically within a framework like Unity [S1, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of CPU-based volume rendering by offloading the classification, edge intersection calculation, and triangle generation to parallel shader cores [S1, unofficial]. Unlike its siblings, this specific implementation relies on a CPU-generated density field (Texture3D) and uses an append buffer for dynamic triangle emission, making it a straightforward but potentially bottlenecked approach for real-time applications where the density field generation is not also GPU-accelerated [S1, unofficial].
 
